From 92f64e73e2f1cadb89a3846f821659200ec5ea30 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitri Geelen <dimitri@geelenandcompany.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 01:25:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Stale task ownership: predicate, attended corrector, and
 teeth (from 832 T-931; AEF T-3568)

tools/_t931-ownership.py classifies every active task: OWNER-JUSTIFIED (an open Human criterion
backs owner: human), OWNER-STALE (none does), NOT-HUMAN-OWNED. --facts prints one TSV line for an
audit rail. It parses criteria with the framework's own lib.delegation (one vocabulary, T-322).
On this tree at 914326e0: 618 active, 391 human-owned, 56 stale.
tools/_t931-ownership-sweep.sh is the corrector: dry run by default, --apply writes ONLY through
'fw task update --owner' (the R-033 path), and it must never run on cron -- an unattended owner
flip is the failure, not the fix. The teeth (16 legs) prove: not wired into any crontab or hook,
writes nothing without --apply, and every write goes through the task-update path.
Your three lessons for T-3568 are the teeth's legs: the corrector never runs on cron; fail-safe
means DO NOT FLIP; audit checks take a facts line from a tool instead of inlining python.
